Hi Nick,

This will be discussed in the upcoming manuscript, but optimized weights are generally good, unless the model assumptions (including stationarity) are violated at the beginnings and ends of gaps, which is where the data is most upweighted. Weights are most useful in three cases: tiny effective sample sizes, very irregular marine data (or similar), and when duty cycling the sampling frequency at a scale not much smaller than the home-range crossing timescale (e.g., first half of the data is 1-hour, second half of the data is 2-hour). Outside of these cases, weights might not be worth the effort if they are slow to calculate or require fiddling with the settings.

As an example of a case where weights might have unintended consequences would be a nesting/denning animal with a tag/collar that doesn't collect data when they are in their nest/den. In this case the full home-range has a lot of density piled up on the nest/den, and the weights will give you some of that whether you want it or not.

As for the AIC question, we have new models coming into the package within the next month and the number of potential models has exploded, so model selection is more intelligent about only considering models with probable features. There is a new level argument in ctmm.select that you can set to level=1 to force all nearby models to be considered. If there are any other models you want, you can always add them to the list or concatenate lists of model fits together before running summary.

Best,
Chris

